*---------------------------------------------------------------------*
* XAOPS  -  ESA/390 INSTRUCTIONS FOR AN ASSEMBLER THAT PREDATES THEM  *
*                                                                     *
* VM/370 CE'S ASSEMBLER IS FROM THE 1970S.  THE CHANNEL SUBSYSTEM     *
* ARRIVED WITH 370-XA IN 1983 AND BSM WITH THE 31-BIT ADDRESSING      *
* FACILITY THE SAME YEAR, SO NONE OF THESE MNEMONICS ARE KNOWN TO IT. *
* RATHER THAN CHANGE THE TOOLCHAIN, EACH ONE IS A MACRO EMITTING ITS  *
* OWN ENCODING.                                                       *
*                                                                     *
* THE S-TYPE ADDRESS CONSTANT  S(EXPR)  PRODUCES THE 4-BIT BASE AND   *
* 12-BIT DISPLACEMENT THAT AN S-FORMAT INSTRUCTION WANTS, RESOLVED    *
* THROUGH THE ACTIVE USING.  SO THESE BEHAVE EXACTLY LIKE THE REAL    *
* INSTRUCTIONS, INCLUDING BASE REGISTER SELECTION.                    *
*                                                                     *
* DS 0H FIRST IN EVERY MACRO: THE S-TYPE CONSTANT FORCES HALFWORD     *
* ALIGNMENT, SO WITHOUT IT A MISALIGNED DC WOULD HAVE A PAD BYTE      *
* INSERTED BETWEEN THE OPCODE AND THE OPERAND.  IN AN INSTRUCTION     *
* STREAM THAT CANNOT NORMALLY HAPPEN, BUT IT COSTS NOTHING TO BE SURE.*
*                                                                     *
* BUILD:   MACLIB GEN XALIB XAOPS                                     *
* USE:     GLOBAL MACLIB XALIB DMSSP CMSLIB                           *
*                                                                     *
* ALL FOUR INSTRUCTIONS NEEDED TO DRIVE A DEVICE -- MSCH, SSCH, TSCH  *
* AND STSCH -- PLUS BSM HAVE BEEN EXECUTED SUCCESSFULLY UNDER HERCULES*
* 3.07 IN ESA/390 MODE FROM HAND-ASSEMBLED BYTES, SO THEIR ENCODINGS  *
* ARE PROVEN BY RUNNING, NOT ONLY BY READING.  SEE THE VERIFICATION   *
* NOTES AT THE END FOR WHICH ARE WHICH.                               *
*---------------------------------------------------------------------*
         MACRO
&LAB     SSCH  &ORB
.*  START SUBCHANNEL.  R1 MUST HOLD X'0001' IN THE HIGH HALFWORD AND
.*  THE SUBCHANNEL NUMBER IN THE LOW.  THE ORB MUST BE WORD ALIGNED
.*  AND -- THIS IS A HERCULES QUIRK, NOT ARCHITECTURE -- 32 BYTES OF
.*  ADDRESSABLE STORAGE MUST EXIST THERE EVEN WHEN THE EXTENSION BIT
.*  IS ZERO AND THE ARCHITECTED BLOCK IS ONLY 12 BYTES LONG.
.*  CC 0 ACCEPTED, 1 STATUS PENDING, 2 BUSY,
.*  CC 3 SUBCHANNEL NOT ENABLED OR LPM AND PAM DISJOINT.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B233',S(&ORB)
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     MSCH  &SCHIB
.*  MODIFY SUBCHANNEL.  READS 28 BYTES -- THE PMCW ONLY.  MANDATORY
.*  BEFORE THE FIRST SSCH ON ANY SUBCHANNEL, BECAUSE A SUBCHANNEL IS
.*  CREATED VALID BUT NOT ENABLED, AND SSCH ON A DISABLED SUBCHANNEL
.*  RETURNS CC 3 WITH NO EXCEPTION AND NO MESSAGE OF ANY KIND.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B232',S(&SCHIB)
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     STSCH &SCHIB
.*  STORE SUBCHANNEL.  THE SAFE WAY TO REACH MSCH IS STSCH, THEN SET
.*  THE ENABLE BIT IN THE STORED PMCW, THEN MSCH -- THAT CANNOT GET
.*  THE RESERVED BITS OF FLAG4, FLAG26 OR FLAG27 WRONG, EACH OF WHICH
.*  IS AN OPERAND EXCEPTION.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B234',S(&SCHIB)
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     TSCH  &IRB
.*  TEST SUBCHANNEL.  STORES 64 BYTES: SCSW, ESW, ECW.  SSCH IS
.*  ASYNCHRONOUS, SO THE FIRST TSCH AFTER IT NORMALLY GIVES CC 1 --
.*  LOOP, AND BOUND THE LOOP SO A STALL IS DIAGNOSABLE.
.*  CC 0 STATUS WAS PENDING AND HAS BEEN CLEARED, 1 NONE PENDING,
.*  CC 3 NOT OPERATIONAL.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B235',S(&IRB)
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     CSCH
.*  CLEAR SUBCHANNEL.  NO STORAGE OPERAND, BUT THE ENCODING STILL
.*  CARRIES A BASE-DISPLACEMENT FIELD, WHICH IS IGNORED.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B230',X'0000'
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     HSCH
.*  HALT SUBCHANNEL.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B231',X'0000'
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     RSCH
.*  RESUME SUBCHANNEL.  THE COUNTERPART OF S/370 RESUME I/O.
.*  OPCODE CONFIRMED BY TWO INDEPENDENT SOURCES -- SEE END OF FILE.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B238',X'0000'
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     TPI   &ADDR
.*  TEST PENDING INTERRUPTION.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B236',S(&ADDR)
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     SAL
.*  SET ADDRESS LIMIT.  IBM ADDED PER-SUBCHANNEL ADDRESS-LIMIT
.*  CHECKING SPECIFICALLY FOR VIRTUAL MACHINES, TO STOP A GUEST'S
.*  CHANNEL PROGRAM REACHING BEYOND ITS ASSIGNED STORAGE.  LIKELY TO
.*  MATTER FOR CP EVENTUALLY.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B237',X'0000'
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     STCRW &ADDR
.*  STORE CHANNEL REPORT WORD.  PART OF THE MACHINERY THAT REPLACES
.*  S/370 CHANNEL LOGOUT, SO IT BELONGS TO THE ERROR-RECOVERY REWRITE
.*  RATHER THAN TO NORMAL I/O.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'B239',S(&ADDR)
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     BSM   &R1,&R2
.*  BRANCH AND SET MODE.  RR FORMAT.
.*
.*  TWO DISTINCT USES, AND THE SECOND IS NOT OBVIOUS:
.*
.*  BSM  0,RX   -- BRANCH TO RX, TAKING THE ADDRESSING MODE FROM ITS
.*                 BIT 0.  ONE MEANS 31-BIT, ZERO MEANS 24-BIT.
.*
.*  BSM  RY,0   -- WITH R2 ZERO NO BRANCH IS TAKEN AT ALL, AND THE
.*                 INSTRUCTION ONLY REPORTS THE CURRENT MODE IN BIT 0
.*                 OF RY, WHICH IS THE SIGN BIT.  SO  SR RY,RY  THEN
.*                 BSM RY,0  THEN  LTR RY,RY  TESTS THE MODE WITHOUT
.*                 NEEDING ANY ADDRESS ABOVE THE 16 MB LINE.  THAT
.*                 MATTERS, BECAUSE EVERY TEST THAT DISTINGUISHES THE
.*                 MODES BY TRUNCATION NECESSARILY USES ONE.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'0B',AL1((&R1)*16+(&R2))
         MEND
         MACRO
&LAB     BASSM &R1,&R2
.*  BRANCH AND SAVE AND SET MODE.  RR FORMAT.  SAVES THE RETURN
.*  ADDRESS IN R1 WITH ITS BIT 0 SET TO THE CALLER'S MODE, SO THE
.*  CALLEE CAN RETURN WITH  BSM 0,R1  AND RESTORE IT.  THIS IS THE
.*  INSTRUCTION A MIXED-MODE CALLING CONVENTION IS BUILT ON.
&LAB     DS    0H
         DC    X'0C',AL1((&R1)*16+(&R2))
         MEND
*---------------------------------------------------------------------*
* VERIFICATION STATUS                                                 *
*                                                                     *
* PROVEN BY EXECUTION.  THESE FIVE RAN CORRECTLY UNDER HERCULES 3.07, *
* ARCHMODE ESA/390, FROM HAND-ASSEMBLED BYTES POKED INTO REAL STORAGE.*
* MSCH ENABLED A SUBCHANNEL, SSCH STARTED A FORMAT-1 CHANNEL PROGRAM, *
* TSCH RETURNED CLEAN STATUS AND THE DEVICE WROTE ITS LINE; BSM       *
* SWITCHED INTO 31-BIT MODE AND BACK, CONFIRMED BOTH BY THE PSW AND BY*
* BSM R1,0 REPORTING THE MODE.                                        *
*                                                                     *
*     MSCH   B232        STSCH  B234        BSM    0B                 *
*     SSCH   B233        TSCH   B235                                  *
*                                                                     *
* NOT YET EXECUTED, BUT CONFIRMED BY A SECOND INDEPENDENT SOURCE --   *
* THE Z390 ASSEMBLER'S OPCODE TABLE (TZ390.JAVA), WHICH IS NOT DERIVED *
* FROM HERCULES:                                                      *
*                                                                     *
*     CSCH   B230        TPI    B236        RSCH   B238               *
*     HSCH   B231        SAL    B237        STCRW  B239               *
*     BASSM  0C                                                       *
*                                                                     *
* THE RSCH DISCREPANCY IS RESOLVED.  AN EARLIER READING OF THE 370-XA *
* REFERENCE SUMMARY GX20-0157-2 GAVE B23B FOR RSCH.  THAT WAS A       *
* CONFUSION BETWEEN TWO SIMILARLY-NAMED CHANNEL INSTRUCTIONS.  Z390'S *
* TABLE LISTS THE WHOLE BLOCK CONTIGUOUSLY:                           *
*                                                                     *
*     B238 RSCH    B239 STCRW   B23A STCPS   B23B RCHP   B23C SCHM    *
*                                                                     *
* B23B IS RCHP -- RESET CHANNEL PATH -- A DIFFERENT INSTRUCTION WITH A*
* RELATED PURPOSE, WHICH IS WHY IT WAS MISREAD AS RSCH.  RSCH IS B238.*
*                                                                     *
* CSCH, HSCH, RSCH AND SAL TAKE NO STORAGE OPERAND -- HERCULES MARKS  *
* THEM ASMFMT_NONE -- SO THEIR LOW HALFWORD IS IGNORED AND IS EMITTED *
* AS X'0000' RATHER THAN THROUGH AN S-TYPE CONSTANT.                  *
*                                                                     *
* HOW TO CHECK ANY OF THEM.  ASSEMBLE A ONE-LINE TEST, THEN READ THE  *
* OBJECT BYTES FROM THE LISTING -- THE FIRST TWO SHOULD MATCH THE     *
* TABLE ABOVE.  OR LOAD THE MODULE UNDER HERCULES WITH  T+  AND LET IT*
* DISASSEMBLE, WHICH IS HOW THE FIVE ABOVE WERE CONFIRMED.            *
*                                                                     *
* NOT INCLUDED, AND WHY                                               *
*                                                                     *
* STOSM AND STNSM NEED NO MACRO.  THEY EXIST IN S/370 EC MODE AND CE'S*
* ASSEMBLER ALREADY KNOWS THEM -- CP ITSELF USES EACH FIVE TIMES.     *
*                                                                     *
* LCTL AND STCTL LIKEWISE: CP USES THEM 172 AND 28 TIMES.             *
*                                                                     *
* SAM24, SAM31 AND SAM64 ARE Z/ARCHITECTURE, NOT ESA/390.  IN ESA/390 *
* THE ADDRESSING MODE IS CHANGED WITH BSM OR BASSM.                   *
*                                                                     *
* IPTE, IVSK AND TPROT ARE ESA/390 DAT INSTRUCTIONS THAT CE'S         *
* ASSEMBLER WILL NOT KNOW EITHER, BUT NOTHING NEEDS THEM YET.  ADD    *
* THEM WHEN THE DAT WORK REACHES THE POINT OF INVALIDATING SINGLE     *
* PAGE TABLE ENTRIES.                                                 *
*---------------------------------------------------------------------*
